                <pb facs="0624" n="512" sameAs="frus:frus1910/pg512"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Governor of Virginia to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Commonwealth of Virginia</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Governor’s Office</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Richmond</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1910-04-11"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">April 11,
                    1910</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In connection with the letter addressed to
                    you on the 9th instant by Col. James Mann, I deem it proper to advise you
                    officially that by an act of the general assembly, approved March 17, 1910,
                    provision was made for the presentation by the people of Virginia to the people
                    of France, “as an expression of Virginia’s cordial admiration and loving
                    regard,” of a reproduction in bronze of the famous statue of Gen. Washington,
                    made by Houdon, which stands in the rotunda of the capitol of Virginia.</p>
                <p>Pursuant to authority vested in me by said act I have appointed as
                    representatives of the people of Virginia, to proceed to France and make
                    presentation of this statue, Col. James Mann, chairman, Norfolk, Va.; Hon. Don
                    P. Halsey, Lynchburg, Va.; and Hon. F. W. King, Clifton Forge, Va.</p>
                <p>Any assistance you may render the gentlemen of this commission in the performance
                    of their pleasant duties will be much appreciated by me.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to remain, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wm. Hodges Mann.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
